# 📡 MINI RADAR

|--> 1. THE PROBLEM:
Radar systems are used to detect objects and determine their distance and position.
In this project, we explored how a simple embedded system can detect objects at different distances and scan an area using a rotating sensor.

|--> 2. Solution:

An Arduino-controlled mini radar system was developed using an ultrasonic sensor and a servo motor.

The ultrasonic sensor measures the distance to objects, while the servo motor rotates the sensor across different angles.

The Arduino processes the sensor readings and sends the angle and distance data through the Serial Monitor.

|--> 3. Why Did We Make It?

It was designed as a practical building project during workshops.

Instead of learning sensors, servo motors, and Arduino programming separately, students combined them into one working system.

During the process of making the project, students:

. Learned how ultrasonic sensors measure distance
. Learned how servo motors control angular movement
. Practiced Arduino programming
. Learned how to read sensor data
. Worked with circuit wiring
. Learned how sensors and actuators interact
. Built a simple scanning system

|--> 4. Workshop Process

The whole process took place gradually in the workshop.

01 — Understanding the Ultrasonic Sensor

Students learned how the HC-SR04 sensor measures the distance between the sensor and an object.

02 — Introduction to the Servo Motor

The servo motor was introduced as an actuator capable of rotating the sensor to different angles.

03 — Creating the Circuit

Students connected the Arduino, ultrasonic sensor, and servo motor and learned how to follow the circuit connections.

04 — Testing the Sensor

The ultrasonic sensor was tested independently to make sure distance measurements were being received correctly.

05 — Controlling the Servo

Students programmed the servo motor to rotate through different angles.

06 — Combining the System

Finally, the ultrasonic sensor and servo motor were integrated into one scanning system.

|--> 5. How It Works

The servo motor rotates the ultrasonic sensor across a range of angles.

At each angle, the HC-SR04 measures the distance to the nearest detected object.

The Arduino then sends the angle and distance values through the Serial Monitor.

This creates a simple implementation of:

||--> Sense → Process → Act

Sense:
The ultrasonic sensor measures distance.

Process:
The Arduino reads and processes the sensor measurement.

Act:
The servo motor changes the sensor's scanning angle.

